tion of the guardrails integration's GA readiness. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E006 | Rule 3 | Default enablement for entitled clusters presumes GA-bar readiness; the assessment determines whether blockers exist that change the enablement scope. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 13 (consumer of artifact) | The TrustyAI team's end-to-end validation verifies the supported integration delivered by the Llama Stack distribution work; the validation consumes that integration as its test subject, so it is serial after it. |\n| E002 → E007 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by all Implementation epics; docs describe what was built, including the versioned API surface and upgrade path. |\n| E003 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same — migration and support documentation reflects the delivered support posture. |\n| E004 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same — the supported guardrails integration must be documented as supported. |\n| E005 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same — validation outcomes (supported configurations, limitations) feed the documentation. |\n| E006 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same — docs describe the default Dashboard experience. |\n\nNon-edges (deliberate):\n- E002 ∥ E003 (Rule 15): same component and team but distinct logical changes\n  (API hardening engineering vs. support policy definition); no shared\n  artifact — parallel.\n- E002/E003 ∥ E004/E006 (Rule 14): independent work areas, no shared\n  build-against artifacts — parallel.\n- No edge from E002/E003 to E006: the timing of flipping the Dashboard\n  default is a release-coordination decision, not a build dependency; the\n  Dashboard change does not build against the API-stability artifacts.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1 (GA-readiness assessment) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 |\n| HLR-2 (API stability guarantee) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 |\n| HLR-3 (production support posture) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 |\n| HLR-4 (TrustyAI guardrails supported integration) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E004, RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 |\n| HLR-5 (Dashboard default enablement) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 |\n| HLR-6 (GA documentation uplift) | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 |\n\nAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- All components are canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`\n  (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard, Documentation) — no non-obvious mappings.\n- E004's work spans Llama Stack (the distribution that promotes the\n  integration from experimental flag to supported) and TrustyAI (the backend);\n  the single most-relevant canonical component is **Llama Stack**, owned by\n  RHOAI GenAI. The TrustyAI-side validation is a separate epic (E005)\n  because a different team (TrustyAI) validates that component — the\n  passive-component exception in Step 2.\n- E007 uses canonical component **Documentation**. No architecture context\n  file exists for Documentation; owning team assigned as RHOAI GenAI (the\n  component graduating to GA) — see Health Warnings.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy provides only a bullet list of\n  affected components (no owner-team or change detail), and has no Impacted\n  Teams table, Dependencies table, acceptance criteria (Given/When/Then), or\n  NFRs. Teams were derived from `.context/architecture-context/` owning teams\n  (RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI, RHOAI Dashboard). Owners should confirm.\n- **Priority inversion flag (informational)**: HLR-6 is P2 in the strategy,\n  but the docs-authoring epic derives P0 from the strategy's `priority:\n  Critical` frontmatter per the docs-authoring priority exception. This is\n  rule-compliant, not an error, but reviewers should be aware the HLR label\n  and the epic priority intentionally differ.\n- **Scope trap (mild)**: HLR-2's \"tested upgrade path from the last two TP\n  releases\" implies upgrade-test infrastructure whose existence is not\n  confirmed in the strategy. It is captured as an acceptance criterion on\n  E002 rather than a separate epic; if the assessment (E001) finds no such\n  infrastructure exists, that epic's scope rewrites accordingly.\n- **Open Question 1** (GA scope unknown until assessment) is modeled as the\n  E001 investigation gating all implementation epics with `rewrite` impact —\n  not a bounded set of outcomes, so no conditional branch epics were created;\n  this is a gated/phased decomposition.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing; the\nonly gate is the internal GA-readiness assessment, modeled as a blocking\nInvestigation (E001) rather than a tiered delivery split. Partial value\nbefore E001 lands is not meaningful because E001 defines the GA scope itself.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32644,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a628f8365d64072b","agent_id":"abd6aadf590dfc4cd","session_id":"7bc7c06b-886c-4c94-b316-537b910a3f9b","uuid":"e206ea45-5a85-4c18-8e13-64fbb3b35e00","timestamp":"2026-10-22T01:18:05.856Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9014 into epics"}
