  the GA-readiness assessment (the strategy's problem statement\n51\t  specifically calls out quality gaps that TP status excuses).\n52\t- Define the supported integration surface: which TrustyAI guardrail\n53\t  capabilities are supported through the Llama Stack safety provider\n54\t  interface, and any documented limitations.\n55\t- Document the integration for GA: configuration steps, supported\n56\t  capabilities, and limitations, as part of the Llama Stack GA\n57\t  documentation set.\n58\t- Coordinate with the TrustyAI team's end-to-end validation of the\n59\t  integration and resolve or document issues it surfaces.\n60\t\n61\t## Acceptance Criteria\n62\t\n63\t- A GA Llama Stack distribution can be configured with TrustyAI safety\n64\t  guardrails without any experimental or TP opt-in flag.\n65\t- The integration is documented as supported, including configuration,\n66\t  supported capabilities, and known limitations.\n67\t- All guardrails-integration gaps classified as must-fix-for-GA by the\n68\t  GA-readiness assessment are remediated or explicitly deferred with\n69\t  stakeholder sign-off recorded in the assessment's scope document.\n70\t- Functional testing of the guardrails integration passes against a\n71\t  TrustyAI service instance in a GA-distribution configuration.\n72\t\n73\t## HLR Traceability\n74\t\n75\t- HLR-4 (P1): Safety guardrails via TrustyAI are a supported, documented\n76\t  integration (not an experimental flag) for GA distributions. This epic\n77\t  is the primary delivery of HLR-4 on the Llama Stack distribution side;\n78\t  the TrustyAI-side validation of the same integration is tracked\n79\t  separately by the TrustyAI team.\n80\t\n81\t## Notes\n82\t\n83\t- Scope is gated by the GA-readiness assessment: whether the integration\n84\t  can be promoted as-is or needs GA-bar remediation is an assessment\n85\t  outcome; if the assessment finds the gap larger than estimated, this\n86\t  epic's scope is rewritten accordingly.\n87\t- This epic's work spans two strategy components (Llama Stack, where the\n88\t  integration is wired and supported, and TrustyAI, the guardrails\n89\t  backend). Llama Stack is the most-relevant canonical component because\n90\t  the change — removing the experimental flag and supporting the\n91\t  integration — lands in the distribution.\n92\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a85b5de9eb80b712","agent_id":"afa05c7d191f9514a","session_id":"7bc7c06b-886c-4c94-b316-537b910a3f9b","uuid":"fcac4955-7ae2-4dcf-b940-d888850e7de7","timestamp":"2026-10-22T01:26:34.871Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4b3cf7502d079ef","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
