ng policy: how API\n50\t  changes are versioned, how deprecations are announced, and how long\n51\t  deprecated surfaces remain supported.\n52\t- Remediate API-stability gaps classified as must-fix-for-GA by the\n53\t  GA-readiness assessment.\n54\t- Build and execute upgrade-path tests from the last two TP releases to the\n55\t  GA release (including whatever upgrade-test infrastructure proves\n56\t  necessary), producing repeatable evidence.\n57\t- Record the stability guarantee statement for the GA API surface as a\n58\t  normative engineering commitment, delivered as input to the GA\n59\t  documentation workstream for user-facing authoring.\n60\t\n61\t## Acceptance Criteria\n62\t\n63\t- The published API surface is versioned, and the versioning scheme is\n64\t  documented.\n65\t- A deprecation policy is defined as normative policy content and\n66\t  consistently applied: any TP API surface not carried into GA is either\n67\t  preserved per policy or explicitly deprecated with a migration path.\n68\t- A tested upgrade path from each of the last two TP releases to the GA\n69\t  release exists, with repeatable test evidence (automated where feasible).\n70\t- All API-stability gaps classified as must-fix-for-GA by the GA-readiness\n71\t  assessment are remediated or explicitly deferred with stakeholder\n72\t  sign-off recorded in the assessment's scope document.\n73\t- The stability guarantee statement is recorded as a normative engineering\n74\t  commitment alongside the GA API surface, as input to the GA\n75\t  documentation workstream (user-facing authoring happens there).\n76\t\n77\t## HLR Traceability\n78\t\n79\t- HLR-2 (P0): Published API surface carries a stability guarantee —\n80\t  versioned APIs, deprecation policy, and a tested upgrade path from the\n81\t  last two TP releases. This epic is the direct delivery of HLR-2.\n82\t\n83\t## Notes\n84\t\n85\t- Scope is gated by the GA-readiness assessment: the definitive set of\n86\t  must-fix stability gaps (and